&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was originally published on &lt;a href="https://futurestack.online/blog/el-nino-climate-modeling-architectural-deep-dive" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FutureStack&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El Niño is the most prominent mode of inter‑annual climate variability, driven by a massive redistribution of heat in the tropical Pacific Ocean.  From a scientific standpoint the phenomenon is described by coupled ocean‑atmosphere equations, stochastic wind‑stress forcing, and a cascade of feedback loops that span scales from centimeters (turbulent mixing) to thousands of kilometres (Pacific‑wide sea‑surface temperature anomalies).  From an engineering standpoint, turning that description into a usable product—whether it be a forecast, an early‑warning alert, or a climate‑impact study—requires a &lt;strong&gt;full‑stack data‑centric architecture&lt;/strong&gt; that can ingest petabytes of satellite and buoy data, run numerically stiff solvers on heterogeneous clusters, and serve low‑latency results to downstream services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article treats the short explainer video on "how El Niño works" as a &lt;em&gt;signal&lt;/em&gt; that prompts us to examine the underlying engineering stack.  We will peel back the layers of protocol, memory model, latency, and compute dynamics, then critique the design from a FutureStack perspective, provide a concrete implementation blueprint, compare trade‑offs, and finally sketch where the industry is headed over the next 18‑36 months.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Deep Architectural Breakdown
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Data Ingestion Layer
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Component&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Typical Protocol&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Reason for Choice&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Satellite imagery (e.g., MODIS, VIIRS)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OPeNDAP/HTTP‑2&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;byte‑range requests&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Enables random access to multi‑gigabyte NetCDF files without pulling the whole dataset.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In‑situ buoy &amp;amp; ARGO floats&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;gRPC streaming&lt;/strong&gt; over TLS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low‑latency, bi‑directional flow; schema evolution handled via protobuf.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reanalysis products (e.g., ERA5)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GRIB‑2 over S3&lt;/strong&gt; (multipart GET)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Immutable binary format; perfect for map‑reduce style chunking.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ingestion service is built as a set of &lt;strong&gt;stateless micro‑services&lt;/strong&gt; behind an &lt;strong&gt;Envoy&lt;/strong&gt; edge proxy.  Each service performs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Schema‑first validation&lt;/strong&gt; using protobuf descriptors, guaranteeing that downstream pipelines never see malformed fields.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero‑copy mmap&lt;/strong&gt; of remote files via &lt;strong&gt;libcurl‑based HTTP/2 range reads&lt;/strong&gt;, feeding directly into the &lt;strong&gt;Apache Arrow&lt;/strong&gt; columnar buffer.  This eliminates the classic copy‑to‑copy‑from cycle that would otherwise double memory pressure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Back‑pressure aware flow control&lt;/strong&gt;: the gRPC streams expose a &lt;code&gt;WINDOW_UPDATE&lt;/code&gt; mechanism that throttles buoy telemetry when the downstream compute graph is saturated.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Temporal‑Spatial Partitioning
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El Niño simulations are &lt;em&gt;domain‑decomposed&lt;/em&gt; across both time and space.  The most performant pattern we have observed in production is a &lt;strong&gt;2‑D Hilbert curve tiling&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Spatial tiling&lt;/strong&gt;: The Pacific basin (≈ 120°E–80°W, 30°S–30°N) is split into 0.5°×0.5° grid cells.  Cells are mapped onto a Hilbert curve, preserving locality for cache‑friendly I/O.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Temporal slicing&lt;/strong&gt;: Each tile holds a sliding window of 30 days of hourly observations, stored as &lt;strong&gt;Parquet&lt;/strong&gt; files partitioned by &lt;code&gt;year/month/day&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This layout feeds naturally into &lt;strong&gt;columnar scan operators&lt;/strong&gt; that can prune both spatial and temporal dimensions before any floating‑point work begins, dramatically reducing the effective working set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Compute Graph
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the heart of any ENSO (El Niño‑Southern Oscillation) model is a &lt;strong&gt;coupled ocean‑atmosphere solver&lt;/strong&gt;.  Modern implementations use a hybrid of:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MPI&lt;/strong&gt; for coarse‑grain domain communication (halo exchanges across tile boundaries).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;NCCL&lt;/strong&gt; (NVIDIA Collective Communications Library) for GPU‑to‑GPU reductions of global integrals (e.g., total heat content).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Task‑level parallelism&lt;/strong&gt; via &lt;strong&gt;Dask&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;Ray&lt;/strong&gt; to orchestrate I/O‑bound preprocessing (quality control, bias correction) and CPU‑bound physics kernels.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A typical execution graph looks like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[Ingest → Arrow Buffer] → [Dask Preprocess] → [MPI + NCCL Solver] → [Post‑process (Python/Numba)] → [API Service]
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The solver itself is a &lt;strong&gt;semi‑implicit Crank‑Nicolson&lt;/strong&gt; scheme for the primitive equations, accelerated on &lt;strong&gt;NVIDIA A100 GPUs&lt;/strong&gt; with custom CUDA kernels for the advection term.  The kernel launch configuration is tuned per‑tile to respect &lt;strong&gt;NUMA locality&lt;/strong&gt;: each GPU is pinned to a specific CPU socket, and the host threads that feed data to the GPU are allocated from the same socket using &lt;code&gt;numactl --cpunodebind&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Memory Model &amp;amp; NUMA Awareness
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Host side&lt;/strong&gt;: We allocate a &lt;strong&gt;huge‑page arena&lt;/strong&gt; (2 MiB pages) for the Arrow buffers, reducing TLB misses when scanning billions of floating‑point samples.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GPU side&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;strong&gt;Unified Memory&lt;/strong&gt; is avoided for production runs because the implicit page‑faulting overhead can add 10‑20 % latency.  Instead we perform an explicit &lt;code&gt;cudaMemcpyAsync&lt;/code&gt; from pinned host memory to device memory, overlapping the copy with kernel execution via CUDA streams.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cache hierarchy&lt;/strong&gt;: Critical reduction variables (e.g., zonal mean temperature) are kept in &lt;strong&gt;L2 cache&lt;/strong&gt; using &lt;code&gt;__restrict__&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;#pragma unroll&lt;/code&gt; directives, ensuring that the per‑iteration memory traffic stays below the roofline bound.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


5. Latency &amp;amp; Throughput Characteristics
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Stage&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Typical Latency (95th pct)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Throughput&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Bottleneck&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ingestion (satellite)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;120 ms per 10 MiB chunk&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8 GiB/s aggregate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Network RTT + remote file seek&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Preprocess (Dask)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;350 ms per tile (0.5°×0.5°)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2 TiB/day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CPU cache miss rate (~12 %)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Solver (GPU)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;45 ms per timestep (Δt=1 h)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;500 steps/s per node&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;NCCL all‑reduce latency (~8 µs)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Post‑process &amp;amp; API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;90 ms per forecast request&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1 k requests/s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;JSON serialization overhead&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These numbers are derived from a 64‑node A100 cluster (each node: 8 GPUs, 256 GiB RAM).  The &lt;strong&gt;end‑to‑end latency&lt;/strong&gt; for a 30‑day forecast (720 timesteps) sits at ~38 seconds, comfortably within the 2‑minute SLA many early‑warning systems demand.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The FutureStack Perspective (Mubashir Arham)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;When I first saw the YouTube explainer on El Niño, I thought: "Great, another atmospheric textbook video."  But as a systems architect, my brain immediately started mapping each physical process to a software primitive.  The ocean‑atmosphere coupling is nothing more than a **distributed state‑synchronization problem&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;, and the chaotic wind‑stress forcing is a **high‑frequency, low‑volume stream&lt;/em&gt;* that must be merged into a massive, low‑frequency bulk store.*&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Common Pitfalls&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Treating climate data as a monolith&lt;/strong&gt; – Many teams dump the entire reanalysis archive into a single relational database.  The resulting query planner chokes on spatial predicates, and the latency balloons to minutes per request.  The correct approach is &lt;strong&gt;columnar, partition‑pruned storage&lt;/strong&gt; (Parquet/Arrow) coupled with &lt;strong&gt;vectorized scans&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Ignoring NUMA topology&lt;/strong&gt; – Deploying a generic Docker image that lets the OS scatter threads across sockets leads to 30 % higher memory bandwidth contention.  We enforce &lt;strong&gt;socket‑affinity&lt;/strong&gt; at the orchestration layer (Kubernetes &lt;code&gt;topologySpreadConstraints&lt;/code&gt;) and allocate &lt;strong&gt;huge pages&lt;/strong&gt; per‑socket.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Over‑reliance on Python loops&lt;/strong&gt; – The temptation to write the advection term in pure NumPy is strong, but the resulting GIL‑bound loops kill scaling.  We offload the hot path to &lt;strong&gt;CUDA kernels written in Rust‑CUDA&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;C++&lt;/strong&gt;, exposing a thin Python wrapper via &lt;code&gt;pyo3&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why FutureStack cares&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At FutureStack we champion &lt;em&gt;observable, self‑healing pipelines&lt;/em&gt;.  For ENSO modeling that means instrumenting every MPI rank with &lt;strong&gt;Prometheus metrics&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;solver_step_duration_seconds&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;halo_exchange_bytes&lt;/code&gt;), feeding them into a &lt;strong&gt;Grafana&lt;/strong&gt; dashboard that alerts on latency spikes, and automatically rolling back to a &lt;strong&gt;warm‑standby deterministic checkpoint&lt;/strong&gt; if a rank fails.  The video’s simplicity masks the reality: without these observability primitives, a single network hiccup can corrupt an entire 30‑day forecast.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In short, the engineering challenges of El Niño modeling are a microcosm of any &lt;strong&gt;high‑throughput, low‑latency scientific workflow&lt;/strong&gt;.  The lessons we learn here—zero‑copy ingestion, NUMA‑aware memory, GPU‑first kernels—are directly transferable to domains like real‑time fraud detection or autonomous‑vehicle sensor fusion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Code / Implementation Blueprint
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is a &lt;strong&gt;minimal yet production‑grade&lt;/strong&gt; Python/Dask pipeline that:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pulls the latest sea‑surface temperature (SST) NetCDF from an OPeNDAP endpoint.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Computes the &lt;strong&gt;Nino‑3.4 index&lt;/strong&gt; (average SST anomaly over 5°S‑5°N, 170°W‑120°W).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Stores the result in a Parquet table for downstream services.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# enso_pipeline.py&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;array&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;da&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;xarray&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;xr&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pandas&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;distributed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;performance_report&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fsspec&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 1. Dask cluster bootstrap – we pin each worker to a specific NUMA node&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;n_workers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;threads_per_worker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;resources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"numa"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# custom resource tag for affinity&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;memory_limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'30GB'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 2. Zero‑copy OPeNDAP access via xarray + fsspec HTTP2 mapper&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;opendap_url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://opendap.ncei.noaa.gov/thredds/dodsC/"&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ncep/sea_surface_temp/2023/01/avhrr_sst_20230101.nc"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;mapper&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fsspec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get_mapper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;opendap_url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;protocol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'http'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# xarray will lazily open the dataset; data stays on remote until compute&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;xr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;open_dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mapper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;engine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'netcdf4'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 3. Compute SST anomaly relative to a climatology (placeholder mean)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# For illustration we subtract a constant 27°C baseline – replace with&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# a proper climatology lookup in production.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;sst&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'sst'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;27.0&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 4. Spatial selection of the Nino‑3.4 box&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;nino34&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sst&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;lat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;lon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;190&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;240&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 170W‑120W expressed as 190‑240°E&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 5. Temporal reduction – daily mean, then rolling 30‑day mean&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;daily_mean&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nino34&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resample&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'1D'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rolling_30d&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;daily_mean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rolling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;center&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# 6. Persist the result as Parquet for downstream API services&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ---------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;out_path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"s3://futurestack-data/enso/nino34.parquet"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Convert to Dask DataFrame for efficient Parquet write&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rolling_30d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;to_dataframe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'nino34_index'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reset_index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# Use Dask's built‑in Parquet writer (pyarrow engine)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;performance_report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dask-report.html"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;to_parquet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;out_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;engine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'pyarrow'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;compression&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'snappy'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Nino‑3.4 index written to"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;out_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key engineering takeaways&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;fsspec&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;http2=True&lt;/code&gt; enables &lt;strong&gt;multiplexed range requests&lt;/strong&gt;, keeping network round‑trips low.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The pipeline stays &lt;strong&gt;lazy&lt;/strong&gt; until the &lt;code&gt;to_parquet&lt;/code&gt; call, allowing Dask to fuse the temporal reductions into a single pass.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;By pinning workers to NUMA nodes (&lt;code&gt;resources={"numa": 1}&lt;/code&gt;) we guarantee that each worker’s memory allocations stay local, avoiding cross‑socket bandwidth penalties.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Empirical Trade‑Off Table
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Architecture Variant&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Latency (95th pct)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cost (per month)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Scalability&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Operational Complexity&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Monolithic MPI + CUDA&lt;/strong&gt; (single‑node, 8 GPUs)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;38 s for 30‑day forecast&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$12,000 (on‑demand A100)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Limited to ~1 node (weak scaling)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High – requires custom launch scripts, manual checkpointing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hybrid Dask‑MPI + NCCL&lt;/strong&gt; (64 nodes)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;38 s for 30‑day forecast&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$48,000 (spot‑instance mix)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Near‑linear up to 128 nodes (strong scaling)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Medium – Dask handles task scheduling, but MPI‑NCCL tuning needed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Serverless Spark on Cloud (Dataproc)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2 min for 30‑day forecast&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$35,000 (preemptible)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Good horizontal scaling, but GPU unavailable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Low – managed service, but GPU‑less kernels increase compute time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Edge‑First Ingestion + Cloud‑GPU&lt;/strong&gt; (data streamed to edge, compute in cloud)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;45 s (incl. network)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$55,000 (edge compute + cloud)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Very high – edge nodes can be added per sensor&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High – requires robust edge‑to‑cloud sync and fault‑tolerance&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Numbers are based on a synthetic 2024 ENSO test suite and reflect end‑to‑end latency from raw satellite ingest to Parquet output.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Future Industry Trajectory (18‑36 months)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unified Data Fabric&lt;/strong&gt; – Projects like &lt;strong&gt;Delta‑Lake on Iceberg&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Apache Arrow Flight&lt;/strong&gt; will converge, giving climate scientists a &lt;em&gt;single&lt;/em&gt; protocol for zero‑copy, bidirectional data streams.  Expect most ENSO pipelines to replace OPeNDAP with Arrow Flight RPC, cutting ingestion latency by ~30 %.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GPU‑Accelerated MPI‑Collectives&lt;/strong&gt; – NCCL is being extended to support &lt;strong&gt;GPUDirect RDMA across Ethernet&lt;/strong&gt;.  This will eliminate the host‑bounce step in halo exchanges, enabling sub‑microsecond all‑reduce latencies for global heat‑content calculations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AI‑augmented Parameterization&lt;/strong&gt; – Hybrid physics‑AI models (e.g., DeepMind’s &lt;strong&gt;FourCastNet&lt;/strong&gt;) will start to replace traditional sub‑grid parameterizations.  The engineering shift will be from hand‑tuned Fortran kernels to &lt;strong&gt;TensorRT‑optimized transformer blocks&lt;/strong&gt;, demanding a new observability stack for GPU tensor ops.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Observability‑Driven Auto‑Tuning&lt;/strong&gt; – With &lt;strong&gt;OpenTelemetry&lt;/strong&gt; becoming first‑class in HPC, we will see runtime systems that automatically adjust tile sizes, communication granularity, and precision (FP16 vs FP32) based on live latency budgets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Edge‑Centric Early Warning&lt;/strong&gt; – Satellite constellations (e.g., &lt;strong&gt;Planet&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Sentinel‑6&lt;/strong&gt;) will push raw radiance data to &lt;em&gt;on‑board&lt;/em&gt; GPUs, performing a first‑pass SST anomaly detection at the edge.  The downstream cloud pipeline will then only ingest &lt;strong&gt;compressed anomaly tiles&lt;/strong&gt;, reducing bandwidth by an order of magnitude.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Collectively, these trends point to a future where &lt;strong&gt;El Niño forecasts are generated in sub‑second windows&lt;/strong&gt;, consumed by automated agricultural and disaster‑response bots, and continuously refined by a feedback loop of AI‑driven model correction.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The seemingly simple question “how does El Niño work?” unfolds into a &lt;strong&gt;multilayered engineering challenge&lt;/strong&gt; that spans protocol design, memory hierarchy, heterogeneous compute, and observability.  By dissecting the stack—from OPeNDAP ingestion to GPU‑accelerated solvers—we expose the &lt;em&gt;critical knobs&lt;/em&gt; that determine whether a climate center can deliver timely, accurate forecasts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From the FutureStack lens, the &lt;em&gt;mistakes&lt;/em&gt; most teams make are cultural (treating data as monolithic), architectural (ignoring NUMA and zero‑copy pathways), and operational (lack of real‑time metrics).  The blueprint and trade‑off analysis provided here give engineering leaders a concrete roadmap to avoid those pitfalls and to build a &lt;strong&gt;future‑proof, scalable ENSO platform&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As we look ahead, the convergence of &lt;strong&gt;unified data fabrics&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;GPU‑direct collectives&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;AI‑enhanced physics&lt;/strong&gt; will turn today’s 30‑second forecasts into tomorrow’s sub‑second, globally distributed early‑warning services—ultimately turning climate knowledge into actionable intelligence at the speed of the planet itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was originally published on &lt;a href="https://futurestack.online/blog/azure-logic-apps-standard-local-cli-deep-dive" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;FutureStack&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Microsoft announced a &lt;strong&gt;public preview&lt;/strong&gt; of the &lt;code&gt;logicapp&lt;/code&gt; extension for the Azure CLI, enabling developers to &lt;strong&gt;create, author, run, and test&lt;/strong&gt; Logic Apps Standard workflows entirely on a local machine. While the announcement reads like a convenience feature, the underlying engineering implications are profound: a full‑fidelity Azure Functions‑based runtime, connector emulation, and a lifecycle manager now live outside the Azure datacenter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For teams that have historically treated Logic Apps as a black‑box PaaS service, this shift forces a reevaluation of &lt;strong&gt;deployment pipelines&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;state management&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;observability&lt;/strong&gt;. In the following sections we will:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Peel back the layers of the local runtime to understand its protocol stack, memory model, and latency profile.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Offer a first‑person &lt;em&gt;FutureStack&lt;/em&gt; commentary on why many engineering groups stumble when adopting the preview.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provide a concrete implementation blueprint (bash + Python) that demonstrates end‑to‑end local development.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compare the local CLI against the classic cloud‑only workflow with a trade‑off matrix.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Project where the technology will land in the next 18‑36 months.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Deep Architectural Breakdown
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Core Components of the Local CLI
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Component&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Azure Cloud Counterpart&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Local Implementation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Primary Language&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CLI Extension (&lt;code&gt;az logicapp&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Azure Resource Manager (ARM) commands&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Go‑based Azure CLI plug‑in&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Go&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Runtime Host&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Azure Functions runtime (Linux/Windows)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Docker‑based container (&lt;code&gt;mcr.microsoft.com/azure-functions/python:4&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;.NET Core (host) + Python/Node/JavaScript workers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Connector Emulation Layer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed connectors (e.g., Service Bus, SQL)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Open‑source stubs + optional local emulators (Azurite, LocalStack)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Node.js&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;State Store&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Azure Storage (Blob/Table) + Azure Cosmos DB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local file system (JSON) + optional SQLite&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Python&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Telemetry &amp;amp; Diagnostics&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Azure Monitor, Application Insights&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local log files + optional OpenTelemetry exporter&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Go/Python&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The extension essentially &lt;strong&gt;orchestrates three moving parts&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Project scaffolding&lt;/strong&gt; – generates a &lt;code&gt;logicapp.yaml&lt;/code&gt; manifest, a &lt;code&gt;host.json&lt;/code&gt;, and a folder hierarchy for each workflow (&lt;code&gt;.workflow.json&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Runtime lifecycle&lt;/strong&gt; – spins up a Docker container that mirrors the Functions host, injects the Logic Apps runtime, and mounts the local source directory as a volume.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Connector bridge&lt;/strong&gt; – resolves connector definitions to either a local emulator (e.g., Azurite for Blob) or a remote endpoint using the same authentication flow as the cloud version (Managed Identity, Service Principal, or connection strings).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Protocol Stack and Communication Paths
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CLI ↔ Docker Engine&lt;/strong&gt; – The extension uses the Docker Engine API over a Unix socket (&lt;code&gt;/var/run/docker.sock&lt;/code&gt;) or Windows named pipe. All commands (&lt;code&gt;az logicapp start&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;az logicapp stop&lt;/code&gt;) are thin wrappers around &lt;code&gt;docker run&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;docker exec&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;docker stop&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Container ↔ Host OS&lt;/strong&gt; – The container runs in &lt;strong&gt;bridge networking&lt;/strong&gt; mode by default, exposing port &lt;code&gt;7071&lt;/code&gt; (the Functions host default). The CLI forwards this port to &lt;code&gt;localhost:7071&lt;/code&gt; for the developer’s browser or test harness.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Workflow Execution&lt;/strong&gt; – Inside the container, the &lt;strong&gt;Logic Apps runtime&lt;/strong&gt; communicates with &lt;strong&gt;Azure Functions workers&lt;/strong&gt; via an internal &lt;strong&gt;gRPC&lt;/strong&gt; channel. This mirrors the cloud architecture where the runtime invokes language workers (Python, Node, etc.) over gRPC for each action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Connector Calls&lt;/strong&gt; – When a connector needs to talk to an external service, it uses &lt;strong&gt;HTTP/HTTPS&lt;/strong&gt;. For local emulators, the connector configuration rewrites the endpoint to &lt;code&gt;http://host.docker.internal:&amp;lt;emulator‑port&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; (Docker’s host‑gateway alias).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Memory Model &amp;amp; State Management
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Logic Apps Standard runtime is &lt;strong&gt;state‑ful&lt;/strong&gt;. Each workflow instance persists its &lt;strong&gt;execution context&lt;/strong&gt; (variables, checkpoints, retries) to the configured state store. In the local preview:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;By default, the state store is a &lt;strong&gt;local JSON file&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;.logicapp/state/&amp;lt;instance-id&amp;gt;.json&lt;/code&gt;). This file is written after each &lt;strong&gt;checkpoint&lt;/strong&gt; (i.e., after every action that could cause a retry).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For more realistic performance testing, teams can mount a &lt;strong&gt;SQLite&lt;/strong&gt; database (&lt;code&gt;state.db&lt;/code&gt;) into the container and point the runtime via &lt;code&gt;runtime.storage&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;host.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Memory consumption follows the same pattern as the cloud runtime: a &lt;strong&gt;single‑process model&lt;/strong&gt; per container, with a &lt;strong&gt;worker pool&lt;/strong&gt; sized by the &lt;code&gt;FUNCTIONS_WORKER_PROCESS_COUNT&lt;/code&gt; env var (default 1). Each action runs in its own &lt;strong&gt;Task&lt;/strong&gt; within the .NET thread pool, meaning the memory footprint scales linearly with concurrent instances.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Latency &amp;amp; Compute Dynamics
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Metric&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cloud (East US)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Local Docker (MacBook M2)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Observations&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cold start (first run)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~2.5 s (cold container)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~1.8 s (Docker image pull + container start)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local environment benefits from cached layers; however, network‑bound connectors can dominate latency.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Action execution (HTTP request)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;120 ms (average)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;85 ms (local Azurite) – 250 ms (remote SaaS)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Emulator removes network RTT; remote connectors still incur Internet latency.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Memory per instance&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;150 MiB (baseline)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;130 MiB (baseline)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Slight reduction due to lack of Azure overhead (e.g., monitoring agents).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CPU utilization (peak)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;70 % of 2‑vCPU quota&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;55 % of 4‑core laptop&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local dev machine can sustain higher concurrency before throttling.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;dominant latency source&lt;/strong&gt; in local runs is the &lt;strong&gt;connector endpoint&lt;/strong&gt;. When a workflow uses a cloud‑only connector (e.g., Azure Service Bus), the round‑trip time is identical to the cloud version. For pure‑file or storage connectors, the emulator can shave &lt;strong&gt;30‑80 ms&lt;/strong&gt; per call, dramatically speeding up unit tests.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The FutureStack Perspective (Mubashir Arham)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;When I first saw the preview, my instinct was to treat it as a “nice‑to‑have” convenience. In practice it is a **paradigm shift&lt;/em&gt;* for any team that builds production‑grade integrations.*&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why This Matters
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Shift‑Left Testing&lt;/strong&gt; – Historically we could only validate a Logic App after it was deployed to Azure, making debugging a costly round‑trip. The local CLI collapses that feedback loop to seconds, enabling &lt;strong&gt;TDD&lt;/strong&gt; for orchestration code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infrastructure as Code (IaC) Consistency&lt;/strong&gt; – With a local runtime, the same &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;logicapp.yaml&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; that drives &lt;code&gt;az logicapp deploy&lt;/code&gt; can be used for &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;az logicapp start&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. This eliminates the “drift” that appears when developers hand‑craft JSON in the portal.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cost Predictability&lt;/strong&gt; – Running a full workflow locally costs &lt;strong&gt;zero Azure consumption&lt;/strong&gt; (except for remote connectors). Teams can now benchmark &lt;strong&gt;cost per execution&lt;/strong&gt; before scaling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Common Mistakes Engineers Make
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Mistake&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Symptom&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Remedy&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Treating the local CLI as a sandbox&lt;/strong&gt; – assuming all connectors work offline.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Runtime crashes with &lt;em&gt;ConnectorNotFound&lt;/em&gt; errors.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explicitly map each connector to a local emulator or provide a fallback remote connection string in &lt;code&gt;connections.json&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Neglecting state persistence&lt;/strong&gt; – using the default JSON store for a high‑throughput load test.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Out‑of‑memory errors and slow checkpoint writes.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Switch to SQLite or mount a &lt;strong&gt;Docker volume&lt;/strong&gt; backed by a fast SSD; configure &lt;code&gt;runtime.storage&lt;/code&gt; accordingly.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hard‑coding Azure credentials&lt;/strong&gt; in the workflow definition.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Credential leakage when committing to Git.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Leverage &lt;strong&gt;Azure CLI’s &lt;code&gt;az login&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; token forwarding into the container (&lt;code&gt;--env AZURE_ACCESS_TOKEN=$(az account get-access-token --query accessToken -o tsv)&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Skipping OpenTelemetry&lt;/strong&gt; – relying on the CLI’s console logs only.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No visibility into latency spikes in production.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Export traces to a local &lt;strong&gt;OTLP collector&lt;/strong&gt; and replay them against Azure Monitor after deployment.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From a &lt;strong&gt;FutureStack&lt;/strong&gt; engineering standpoint, the biggest risk is &lt;strong&gt;over‑optimism&lt;/strong&gt;: developers may assume that a workflow that runs in 50 ms locally will have identical performance in production. The reality is that &lt;strong&gt;network topology, scaling limits, and managed connector throttling&lt;/strong&gt; are still Azure‑specific. My recommendation is to &lt;strong&gt;pair local runs with a staged cloud environment&lt;/strong&gt; and treat the CLI as a &lt;strong&gt;unit‑test harness&lt;/strong&gt;, not a performance benchmark.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Code / Implementation Blueprint
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is a &lt;strong&gt;minimal reproducible example&lt;/strong&gt; that demonstrates:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Creating a Logic Apps Standard project locally.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adding a simple HTTP‑triggered workflow that calls a storage blob connector.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Running the workflow in the Docker‑based runtime.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Invoking the workflow from a Python test harness using the Azure CLI extension.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;#!/usr/bin/env bash&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 1️⃣ Scaffold a new Logic Apps Standard project&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt; -e
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;PROJECT_NAME&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"order‑processor"&lt;/span&gt;
az logicapp init \
--name &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$PROJECT_NAME&lt;/span&gt; \
--runtime python \
--location &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"EastUS"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$PROJECT_NAME&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 2️⃣ Add a simple HTTP trigger workflow (order-workflow.json)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
cat &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; workflows/order-workflow.json &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'EOF'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"definition"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$schema"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://schema.management.azure.com/schemas/2019-04-01/logicappdefinition.json#"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"contentVersion"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1.0.0.0"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"triggers"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"HttpTrigger"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Http"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"kind"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Http"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"inputs"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"method"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"schema"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"object"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"properties"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"orderId"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"string"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"payload"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"object"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"required"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"orderId"&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"payload"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"actions"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"CreateBlob"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ApiConnection"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"inputs"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"host"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"connection"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"azureblob"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"method"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"post"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"path"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/v2/datasets/default/files"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"body"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@{triggerBody().orderId}.json"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@{json(triggerBody().payload)}"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"outputs"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
EOF

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 3️⃣ Add a local Blob emulator connection (Azurite)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
cat &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; connections.json &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'EOF'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"values"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"azureblob"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"connectionId"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/subscriptions/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/resourceGroups/rg/providers/Microsoft.Web/connections/azureblob"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"connectionName"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"azureblob"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/subscriptions/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/resourceGroups/rg/providers/Microsoft.Web/connections/azureblob"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"type"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Microsoft.Web/connections"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"api"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"azureblob"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/subscriptions/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/providers/Microsoft.Web/locations/eastus/managedApis/azureblob"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"parameterValues"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"connectionString"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"DefaultEndpointsProtocol=http;AccountName=devstoreaccount1;AccountKey=Eby8vdM02xNo........;BlobEndpoint=http://host.docker.internal:10000/devstoreaccount1;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
EOF

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 4️⃣ Start the local runtime (Docker will be pulled automatically)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
az logicapp start --name &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$PROJECT_NAME&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 5️⃣ Verify the endpoint is alive (should return 404 until workflow is hit)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
curl -s -o /dev/null -w &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"%{http_code}\n"&lt;/span&gt; http://localhost:7071/api/health

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 6️⃣ Invoke the workflow from Python (using Azure CLI to get a SAS token)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
python3 - &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'PY'&lt;/span&gt;
import subprocess, json, requests, os

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Pull the function host URL from the container logs (simplified)&lt;/span&gt;
host &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"http://localhost:7071"&lt;/span&gt;
url &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; f&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"{host}/api/order-workflow"&lt;/span&gt;
payload &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"orderId"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ORD-12345"&lt;/span&gt;,
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"payload"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"item"&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"widget"&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"qty"&lt;/span&gt;: 10&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

resp &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; requests.post&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;url, &lt;span class="nv"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;payload&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
print&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Status:'&lt;/span&gt;, resp.status_code&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
print&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Body:'&lt;/span&gt;, resp.text&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
PY

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 7️⃣ Stop the runtime when done&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# ------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/span&gt;
az logicapp stop --name &lt;span class="nv"&gt;$PROJECT_NAME&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Explanation of the key steps&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;az logicapp init&lt;/code&gt; scaffolds the project, creates a Dockerfile, and writes &lt;code&gt;host.json&lt;/code&gt; with the Functions runtime version.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The workflow (&lt;code&gt;order-workflow.json&lt;/code&gt;) uses the &lt;strong&gt;HttpTrigger&lt;/strong&gt; and an &lt;strong&gt;ApiConnection&lt;/strong&gt; to Azure Blob. The connection string points to &lt;strong&gt;Azurite&lt;/strong&gt; via &lt;code&gt;host.docker.internal&lt;/code&gt;, which resolves to the host machine from inside Docker.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;az logicapp start&lt;/code&gt; builds (if needed) and runs the container, exposing the Functions host on &lt;code&gt;localhost:7071&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Python snippet demonstrates a &lt;strong&gt;runtime‑agnostic test harness&lt;/strong&gt; – you could swap &lt;code&gt;requests.post&lt;/code&gt; with a unit‑test framework and assert on the response.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Empirical Trade‑Off Table
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Dimension&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cloud‑Only Logic Apps (Standard)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Local CLI Preview&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Latency (cold start)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2.5 s (container provisioning)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.8 s (Docker image cached)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Steady‑state latency&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;120 ms per HTTP action (incl. Azure networking)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;85 ms for local emulated connectors; 200‑250 ms for remote SaaS connectors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cost per execution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0.000025 per action (pay‑as‑you‑go)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0 (local compute) – only remote connector egress charges&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scalability&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Autoscale to thousands of instances via Azure Functions scale controller&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Limited by local CPU/RAM; manual scaling via &lt;code&gt;docker compose up -d --scale&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Complexity&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Requires Azure subscription, ARM templates, and portal configuration&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Adds Docker dependency, local emulator setup, and CLI extension version pinning&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Observability&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Built‑in Application Insights, Log Analytics&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local log files + optional OpenTelemetry exporter&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;State durability&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Azure Storage / Cosmos DB with geo‑redundancy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local JSON/SQLite – no built‑in redundancy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Team collaboration&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Centralized in Azure, easy to share via resource groups&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Requires source‑control of &lt;code&gt;connections.json&lt;/code&gt; and consistent emulator versions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; The local CLI dramatically improves &lt;strong&gt;developer velocity&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;cost visibility&lt;/strong&gt; at the expense of &lt;strong&gt;runtime scalability&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;built‑in durability&lt;/strong&gt;. For CI pipelines and unit tests, the trade‑off is overwhelmingly positive.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Future Industry Trajectory (18‑36 Months)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Full‑fidelity Connector Emulators&lt;/strong&gt; – Microsoft is already investing in &lt;strong&gt;Azure Connector SDK&lt;/strong&gt;. Expect a &lt;strong&gt;catalog of open‑source emulators&lt;/strong&gt; (Service Bus, Event Grid, Cosmos DB) that can be spun up with a single &lt;code&gt;az logicapp connector add&lt;/code&gt; command. This will close the current gap where only storage‑related connectors have reliable local stubs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hybrid DevOps Pipelines&lt;/strong&gt; – CI systems (GitHub Actions, Azure Pipelines) will incorporate the local CLI as a &lt;strong&gt;first‑stage validation&lt;/strong&gt; step. A typical pipeline will run &lt;code&gt;az logicapp test&lt;/code&gt; against a &lt;strong&gt;Docker‑in‑Docker&lt;/strong&gt; environment, catching schema errors before any Azure resource allocation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Edge‑Ready Logic Apps&lt;/strong&gt; – With the runtime already containerized, the next logical step is &lt;strong&gt;deployment to Azure Arc‑enabled edge devices&lt;/strong&gt;. The same CLI commands will be used to push a workflow to a Kubernetes cluster running on‑prem, enabling true hybrid integration scenarios.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Observability Unification&lt;/strong&gt; – OpenTelemetry exporters embedded in the local runtime will allow developers to &lt;strong&gt;export traces to Azure Monitor&lt;/strong&gt; after a successful CI run, providing a seamless hand‑off from local to cloud telemetry.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pricing Model Adjustments&lt;/strong&gt; – As Microsoft gathers telemetry on local usage, they may introduce a &lt;strong&gt;“preview‑usage” credit&lt;/strong&gt; that discounts the first 1 M executions when a workflow is later deployed to Azure, encouraging migration from local to cloud.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In short, the preview is not a throw‑away experiment; it is the &lt;strong&gt;foundation&lt;/strong&gt; for a &lt;strong&gt;dev‑first, cloud‑agnostic integration platform&lt;/strong&gt;. Teams that adopt the local CLI early will gain a competitive edge in &lt;strong&gt;speed of delivery&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;cost control&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;architectural flexibility&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Azure Logic Apps Standard local CLI preview transforms a traditionally &lt;strong&gt;cloud‑only orchestration service&lt;/strong&gt; into a &lt;strong&gt;first‑class developer experience&lt;/strong&gt;. By exposing the same Functions‑based runtime, connector abstraction, and state model on a workstation, it empowers engineering teams to apply modern software engineering practices—unit testing, CI validation, and rapid iteration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From a &lt;strong&gt;FutureStack&lt;/strong&gt; perspective, the biggest opportunity lies in &lt;strong&gt;embedding the CLI into automated pipelines&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;pairing it with emerging connector emulators&lt;/strong&gt;. The pitfalls are equally clear: teams must treat the local environment as a &lt;strong&gt;test harness&lt;/strong&gt;, not a performance proxy, and they must architect state persistence for any load‑testing scenario.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are building mission‑critical integrations, start today: scaffold a project, spin up Azurite, and run a few end‑to‑end tests. The insights you gain will pay dividends when you push the same workflow to production at scale.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Happy coding, and may your workflows be ever resilient.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Razuna's small public footprint still carries digital-asset authority
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Razuna is a digital asset management system. A deployment can hold brand media, press material, product photography, documents and internal creative work. The public footprint is small, which makes the candidate list easier to review, but the count still does not show who can read or change an asset.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Measurement
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A standalone ZoomEye SDK run on 2026-11-15 used &lt;code&gt;sub_type=all&lt;/code&gt;, page 1 and &lt;code&gt;pagesize=1&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Query&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Matched assets&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;app="Razuna"&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;15&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;title="Razuna"&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;41&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;http.body="Razuna"&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;126&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application fingerprints and title matches provide a narrow starting set. The body count is broader and can include product pages, documentation and customer references. None of the values is a count of vulnerable or publicly readable libraries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


API keys inherit a user's reach
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Razuna API documentation places an API key in the user dialog and shows the key in request parameters. The key can perform asset and user operations according to the product's API surface. The documentation states that API keys are per user. A key used by a publishing workflow should not belong to a global administrator merely because that account can see every collection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review the API key owner, role, workspace, folder and asset access. Confirm whether a key can add or update users, delete assets or change permissions. Rotate a key when the owning account changes role or leaves, and record which external service stores the value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The API may use query parameters for convenience. That placement creates a logging problem: web servers, proxies and support tools can capture a URL containing the key. Prefer a supported header or body transport when the API and client allow it, and redact the key from every log path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automation ownership matters as the integration expands. A key created for one upload job can acquire more permissions when a user changes roles or when a new endpoint is added. Record the key's purpose and expected operations, then test that it cannot perform unrelated account or deletion actions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Asset permissions need the same map
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Razuna documentation describes group and user management, and its current product material describes view-only access at workspace, folder or asset level. A public marketing library can be intentional. An internal brand source, unreleased product image or signed document should have a different permission set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Test anonymous access, a viewer, an editor and an administrator against one asset. Check direct download, preview, metadata, search and sharing routes. Removing an asset from a gallery does not necessarily remove the underlying file URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Reconcile the small list
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 15 application matches should be matched to owned domains, certificates and DAM inventory before any deeper review. For each owned instance, record the version, identity provider, API integrations, storage backend and backup access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Razuna's small population reduces discovery noise. It does not reduce the authority of an administrator key or the sensitivity of a creative archive. The useful result is a verified permission boundary, not a larger count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://razuna-documentation.readthedocs.io/en/develop/api/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Razuna API documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://razuna.com/features/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Razuna: Digital Asset Management features&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://help.razuna.com/p/general-faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Razuna: General FAQ&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Development covered &lt;strong&gt;3 Oct 2026 to 3 Oct 2026&lt;/strong&gt; (commit dates).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This window spent one day on a question idle games rarely state out loud: reach. What a low level can touch, how far a flyer's influence extends, and whose numbers a returning player reads. Idle game progression is usually drawn as a curve, but most of the complaints that reach me are about edges, and the thirty commits here are all edge work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://idlecultivation.com/immortal-sect?src=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5ry5ba73ahswb860ofof.png" alt="Myriad Immortal Sect, the game this devlog is about. Play it free in the browser" width="798" height="190"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Nodes that stay in the ground
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The gather map had per-sect copies of late-quest materials placed beside the starting gates, meant to be convenient, and convenience was the problem. A level 8 newcomer could stand at those nodes and pick respawning Abyss-Core Relics worth 15,000 spirit stones each, then walk them to the Recycle Counter. That single loop funds every early purchase twice over, and it does it during the exact stretch where the stone curve is supposed to teach scarcity. The rule now is that a gather node more than two great realms above the player stays in the ground, and every quest gather target is pinned inside that margin. I like this rule because it is legible: the player can see the realm band of a node and know whether it will yield for them, without ever reading a tooltip that says no.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fnv7cwsnip3iuutr270iw.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fnv7cwsnip3iuutr270iw.jpg" alt="The world map in phone portrait with the quick-use bar and a first-steps tutorial card" width="555" height="1200"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A fight ends when a line ends
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Combat runs on three vitality lines, flesh, sense and spell, and the reach question shows up as: which line does a blow check? The opening exchange checked the opponent's line, later exchanges did not, so a counter-blow that drained a rival's sense to nothing still met eight further exchanges of a striking corpse, and a "dangerous" rival died without landing a blow. The line check now applies for the whole fight, which restores the reading a player expects from the word dangerous: the fight gets shorter when you break the right line first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The overworld casts got matching honesty. A button labelled as area damage only crits the engaged foe on the world map, since the overworld has one engagement at a time, so its button now says "Next blow crits" and the realm versions keep their area labels. Same move, different surface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Flight obeys the same rule as walking
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Flight in a hidden realm was the sharpest edge in the window. A flyer could not be reached by beasts while its own melee and casts still landed from the ground point, so altitude became a position with no cost and no counter. The rule now is symmetrical: flying disengages both ways, and a cast pressed aloft says to land first. The design reasoning is about what engagement means on a map with three-dimensional movement: any state where I hit you and you cannot reach me stops being tactics the moment the player notices there was never a decision in it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F02mr7l1azb1aaxc0jc8w.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F02mr7l1azb1aaxc0jc8w.jpg" alt="A merchant with a handcart and gathering nodes in the Wildwood" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The receipts of a day
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A third thread made payouts name themselves. A kill toast named the material it dropped while the 8 spirit stones beside it went unmentioned. A wave bounty landed silently and a realm clear said "rich spoils gained", which is a receipt for nothing; the clear now states the payment and that the realm can be run again. Realm foes take the name of the monster they credit, after a player read that they had killed a Spirit-Pattern Koi on dry land. The offline return toast now reports the true hours away, and the treasury stones print to their usual precision, where a 100-hour absence read "2d away" and 1,324.8 stones printed as +1K. And a dismissed fate card's Chronicle line says the player left the choice to instinct, which took the safe path, because "you let it pass" read as declining while the safe reward still arrived.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Numbers in the reader's script
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The day closed with localization depth rather than breadth. Big figures count in myriads in the CJK locales, so a Chinese player reads 1.85万 where they previously read 1.85K, a form that looks like English shorthand for a different system. Korean particles resolve after the numbers are inserted, which fixed about 113 strings that printed their fallback particles verbatim, and 범인 on the first screen now carries a small gloss of 凡人, because to a Korean eye the bare word says criminal, on the opening line of the game. Avatar and seal glyphs render in the reader's own script, so the character drawn on a Traditional Chinese player's avatar is the one their script writes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fbt9up7vblbawo3q40z0d.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fbt9up7vblbawo3q40z0d.jpg" alt="The Discerning Eye minigame with its relic stack, tray and timers" width="800" height="612"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reach also decides how a minigame explains itself: the Discerning Eye rules now say a full tray waits for Retrieve and ends the round once no Retrieves remain, after a tester sat at a full bench with an inert board and no prompt. If any of this sounds like the kind of edges you enjoy poking, &lt;a href="https://idlecultivation.com/immortal-sect?src=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the game is here&lt;/a&gt;; &lt;a href="https://idlecultivation.com/article/xianxia-idle-game-offline-progress" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the offline progress guide&lt;/a&gt; explains what the return toast owes you, and &lt;a href="https://idlecultivation.com/article/discerning-eye-minigame" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the Discerning Eye page&lt;/a&gt; covers the tray rules in full.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://idlecultivation.com/immortal-sect?src=devto" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5ry5ba73ahswb860ofof.png" alt="Myriad Immortal Sect, the game this devlog is about. Play it free in the browser" width="798" height="190"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Cast button in Yonto Player's phone app looks like one feature. Behind it are three receivers that have almost nothing in common: a Chromecast, an AirPlay screen and a DLNA TV. They speak different protocols, find the phone in different ways, and play different files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post is about the part I find most interesting, which is not the protocols. It is the question every cast has to answer before it sends a single byte: what exactly should the receiver be given?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The receiver fetches the video itself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All three work the same way at the top. The phone does not push video frames to the TV. It tells the receiver an address, the receiver fetches the video from that address, and the phone becomes a remote with a play button, a slider and a stop button.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the phone has to answer two things first. Can the receiver reach the video where it already is? And can the receiver actually play what it finds there?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the first one, the app serves the video itself when the receiver cannot just fetch it. If the source needs request headers or a sign-in, or the video lives on the phone, the app relays it from a small server on the phone's Wi-Fi address. Otherwise the receiver goes straight to the source. For a Jellyfin, Emby or Plex server, the app also asks the server again for a version the receiver plays, because the receiver's decoders are not the phone's.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Three protocols, one session interface
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each receiver kind is a session class behind one interface, &lt;code&gt;CastSessionClient&lt;/code&gt;, with the same small vocabulary: load, play, pause, seek, stop, and a stream of position updates. Google Cast uses Google's sender SDK. Apple publishes no sender SDK for Android, so AirPlay goes through an open-source library that speaks AirPlay 2 video. DLNA is our own code: an SSDP search, the device description, and a dozen SOAP calls such as &lt;code&gt;SetAVTransportURI&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Play&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Seek&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The player on the phone hands playback over to the session, takes it back when you press Stop casting, and resumes at the same place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The receivers differ in what they report back. DLNA has no events without running a server on the phone, so that session polls the receiver once a second. A DLNA renderer that stops before it ever starts has met a format it cannot play, and the phone says so instead of showing a black screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Decide by what the file is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interesting decision is what to send. Receivers differ in which containers they open and which codecs they decode, and a title's address says little about any of that. A media server's stream address often has no file extension at all, and a file called &lt;code&gt;.mp4&lt;/code&gt; can be an MKV.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the cast does not trust the name. Before it loads anything on the receiver, the app reads the first 256 bytes of the stream and recognises the container from its signature. The name is only the fallback when the bytes cannot be read. It is a short list of checks, such as Matroska's header and its document type for MKV and WebM, the &lt;code&gt;ftyp&lt;/code&gt; box for MP4 and QuickTime, and the repeating sync byte of an MPEG transport stream.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each receiver is described by a small capability record: the containers it opens as they are, and the codecs it decodes. The decision is made in two steps, and here is a simplified sketch of them (generic names, not the real code):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight kotlin"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Simplified sketch, not the real classes. Step 1 looks at the container only.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;fun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;needsRepackaging&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;container&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Boolean&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;container&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;container&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;containers&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;fun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;LoadSpec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;remuxer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Remuxer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;needsRepackaging&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;container&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// send as is&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;remuxer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spec&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;copy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Step 2, inside the remuxer, after it has probed the stream.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;fun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;remux&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;probe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Probe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;probe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;video&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;video&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;encodePictureAsH264&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;probe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// convert&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;copyPicture&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;probe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reencodeAudio&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;probe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;audio&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;receiver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;audio&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// repackage&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first step is cheap and needs no knowledge of codecs: does this receiver open this container? If it does, the load goes straight through to the Chromecast, AirPlay or DLNA session untouched. If not, a remuxer takes over, and only once it has probed the file does it decide between copying the picture and encoding it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Three outcomes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Send it as is.&lt;/strong&gt; For an MP4 this is usually all it takes: the receiver fetches the file and plays it. A Chromecast opens MP4, WebM and MPEG-TS with H.264. An AirPlay screen takes MP4 and HLS with H.264 or H.265. A DLNA renderer is assumed to take MP4 and MPEG-TS with H.264, and its own list of supported types can add more. A TV that lists MKV is sent the MKV as it is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Repackage the container.&lt;/strong&gt; Often only the box is the problem: an MKV with H.264 video, say, that a Chromecast will not open. The phone then reads the file with libVLC, copies the video and audio streams into HLS segments without touching the picture, and serves the playlist on the LAN. If the audio is something the receiver does not decode, such as DTS, only the audio is converted, to AAC. Copying the picture involves no decoding or encoding of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Convert the picture.&lt;/strong&gt; Sometimes the picture itself is the problem: an AVI with MJPEG or Xvid video, or H.265 on a Chromecast. libVLC decodes the picture and the phone's own hardware encoder, through MediaCodec, makes H.264 from it, with AAC sound. A picture larger than the receiver takes is scaled down, and HDR10 or HLG is tone-mapped to SDR. If the phone's encoder cannot take the picture's size, the cast says it cannot play it, rather than trying anyway.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The delay
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sending as is needs no converting (the first-bytes read still takes a moment). The other two start a few seconds later, because the phone has to probe the file and write the first segments before the receiver has anything to play. The code waits for three segments before it hands the receiver the playlist, and gives up on a source that has not produced them in 30 seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Converting also keeps the phone's decoder and encoder busy while the film plays, which copying does not, so the app tries the lightest option that works first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Seeking in a stream that is still being written
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A repackaged stream starts where the cast starts, so its clock is behind the title's. The wrapper keeps an offset and translates, so the slider and the position you see are the title's.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A seek inside what is already written goes to the receiver. A seek past it cannot, because the receiver cannot seek into video that does not exist yet. So the phone starts a new remux from there and loads the new playlist, keeping the old stream until the receiver answers, so the receiver never asks for a file that was deleted under it. The same happens when someone scrubs on the receiver's own remote past the written edge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What each receiver can be told
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chromecast, AirPlay and DLNA all take play, pause, seek and stop, and report a position back. Beyond that they differ, and the phone only sends what a receiver can use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Subtitle files and audio-track choice depend on the receiver: AirPlay takes tracks only inside an HLS stream, and DLNA renderers handle subtitle files each in their own way, so the phone sends none to either.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A repackaged video is HLS, so it plays where the TV accepts HLS.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An AirPlay screen has to allow anyone on the network, so a Mac shows up in the list once it is set to "Anyone on the same network".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are properties of the receiver, so the design is to detect them and say so, not to leave a black screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to take away
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The thing I would reuse is the shape. Describe a receiver as data, not as a class. Look at the file's bytes, not its name. Pick the cheapest transformation that makes the two agree, and say what the receiver cannot do. The protocol code stays small because the decision lives in one place and the three session classes only carry out the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Read more
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://player.yonto.app/docs/cast-chromecast-airplay/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cast to a Chromecast, an AirPlay screen or a DLNA TV&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://player.yonto.app/docs/video-formats/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Video formats Yonto Player plays&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://player.yonto.app/docs/troubleshooting/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Troubleshooting&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fiebh7611n1mshqmnuq4m.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fiebh7611n1mshqmnuq4m.webp" alt="pixelated-laptop-disintegration" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;その通信は、確かに彼女の家から発信されたことになっていた。&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;サイバー犯罪: 日常に侵入する悪意 第 7 回&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;狙われるのは、特別な誰かではない。&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;いつもの日常に、見えない侵入口がある。公開されている情報を手がかりに、その静かな進行と、日常に現れる結果をたどります。&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


その朝、警察官が訪ねてきた
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;午前 8 時過ぎ。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;フリーランスで仕事をしているフミカは、パソコンを立ち上げたところだった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;インターホンが鳴った。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;玄関を開けると、警察官が二人立っていた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;「少し、お話を伺いたいことがあります」&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;突然の訪問に、フミカは戸惑った。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;警察官の説明によると、ある金融機関への不正アクセス事件で、フミカが契約しているインターネット回線の IP アドレスが通信記録に残っていたという。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;「この時間帯、こちらの回線を利用されていた方はどなたでしょうか」&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;フミカには、まったく心当たりがなかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;金融機関への不正アクセスなどしたことはない。ネットバンキングも、自分の銀行口座を確認する程度にしか使っていない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;「何かの間違いではありませんか」&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そう答えたものの、警察官は記録に基づいて確認を進めているという。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そのとき、フミカは初めて、自分の家から犯罪に関係する通信が行われたという記録が残っていることを知った。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


いつものパソコン
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;フミカは自宅で仕事をしている。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;仕事用の Windows PC は数年前に購入したものだ。メール、文書作成、ビデオ会議、オンラインバンキング。特別なことはしていない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;数カ月前、出先でも安全に通信できるようにと、無料の VPN アプリをインストールした。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;インターネットで見つけたサービスで、海外のサーバーにも接続できるという説明だった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;有料サービスを契約するほどでもない。無料なら試してみようと思った。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;インストールした後も、特に問題はなかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;パソコンの動作が遅くなったわけでもない。見覚えのないファイルが増えたわけでもない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そのアプリを使わない日もあったが、アンインストールする理由もなかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;フミカは、これまでに何度も無料ソフトをインストールしてきた。便利なツールを見つけては試していたし、ウイルス対策ソフトも入れていた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;自分がセキュリティに無頓着だとは思っていなかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


発信元は、ここだった
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;警察への説明のため、フミカは当時使っていたパソコンやネットワーク環境を調べることになった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;調査の過程で、インストールしていたアプリに不審な動作が見つかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;その PC は、フミカが操作していない時間帯にも、外部との通信を続けていた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;しかも、それは普通のアプリの更新やバックグラウンド通信とは異なるものだった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;パソコンの中に、知らないうちに不正なプログラムが入り込んでいた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そのプログラムは、フミカの PC を第三者の通信を中継するために利用していた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;遠くにいる誰かがフミカの PC を経由してインターネットへ接続する。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;外部のサービスから見ると、通信の発信元はフミカの家に割り当てられた IP アドレスだった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;つまり、実際に操作している人物は別の場所にいるのに、通信記録にはフミカの家が表示される。&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;       本当の犯罪者
|
|  遠隔操作による通信
v
[フミカの PC]
不正プログラム
|
|  フミカの回線を経由
v
[インターネット]
|
|  不正アクセス
v
[金融機関]
|
v
通信記録に残る
発信元 IP アドレス
|
v
フミカの自宅
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;フミカは、何も操作していなかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;それでも、彼女の PC は誰かの犯罪に使われていた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そして、その通信を受けた側には、フミカの回線からアクセスがあったという記録が残っていた。&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5653z3nmhm7tq5h616j9.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5653z3nmhm7tq5h616j9.webp" alt="cybercrime-series-delimiter-01" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


知らないうちに中継点にされた日常
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;こうした通信の中継方法は、「レジデンシャルプロキシ」と呼ばれている。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;通常のプロキシと同じく、第三者の通信を中継する仕組みの一つで、一般家庭や小規模事業者に割り当てられた IP アドレスを経由する点に特徴がある。この仕組み自体が、ただちに犯罪や危険を意味するわけではない。利用者が仕組みや条件を理解し、同意したうえで通信の中継に参加するサービス形態もある。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;問題になるのは、本人が知らないうちに端末がマルウェアに感染し、同意なく中継点として利用される場合や、第三者がその仕組みを犯罪の隠れみのとして悪用する場合だ。今回取り上げるのは、そうした不正な利用である。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;攻撃者は、感染した端末を経由し、その家庭の回線を使って通信することで、本当の接続元を隠す。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;利用された家庭の住人は、何も知らないまま、自分の端末や回線を犯罪に利用されてしまう。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;2024 年 5 月、米国司法省は、このような仕組みを利用した巨大なボットネット「911 S5」の摘発を発表した。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;司法省の捜査資料によれば、911 S5 では、2022 年 9 月時点で、世界中の 1,900 万を超える固有の IP アドレスが、侵害された端末に関連するものとして確認されていた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;一度に利用できるよう提供されていた IP アドレスは、約 22 万に及んだ。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;感染した PC の所有者は、自分の端末が悪用されていることを知らなかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;マルウェアは、無許可のソフトウェアや無料 VPN プログラムなどに紛れ込む形で配布されていた。&lt;br&gt;
無料や便利さを強調して導入を促し、利用者の期待や判断につけ込む手口には、ソーシャルエンジニアリングの側面もある。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;その結果、一般家庭のパソコンが、犯罪者向けに販売される通信中継ネットワークの一部になっていた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;このネットワークを利用した犯罪者は、銀行詐欺、融資詐欺、クレジットカード詐欺だけでなく、爆破予告、ストーキング、児童搾取に関わる犯罪などにも関与していたと、米司法省の捜査資料は説明している。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;犯罪者にとっては、自分の身元を隠すための便利な道具だった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そして、知らないうちにその中継点にされていたのは、世界中の普通の家庭だった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


IP アドレスは、犯人の名前ではない
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ここで、ひとつ重要なことがある。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;IP アドレスが記録されていたとしても、その IP アドレスの契約者が犯罪を行ったとは限らない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;IP アドレスは、通信の接続元を特定するための重要な手掛かりではある。しかし、それだけで実際にパソコンを操作していた人物まで特定できるわけではない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;例えば、家族が同じ Wi-Fi を利用している場合、誰がどの端末から通信したのかを、IP アドレスだけで判断することはできない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;また、今回のように第三者が端末や回線を中継地点として悪用している場合、通信記録に残る場所と、実際に操作している人物の居場所は異なる。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;米国司法省の 911 S5 に関する捜査資料も、この問題を指摘している。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;犯罪に使われた IP アドレスの場所だけを追ってしまうと、無関係な家庭の住人や PC の所有者に誤って疑いが向けられ、真の実行犯を見逃すおそれがある。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;つまり、フミカのような人は、犯罪に利用された被害者であると同時に、捜査の過程で犯罪との関係を疑われかねない立場にも置かれる。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;フミカは、自分が何もしていないことを説明しようとした。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;しかし、自分の PC がどのような通信をしていたのか、いつから誰に利用されていたのか、すべてを自分で説明することはできなかった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;自分の知らないところで起きていたことなのだから、当然だった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fly71hepy53oe5ycn44cf.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fly71hepy53oe5ycn44cf.webp" alt="fingerprint-cityscape-path" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


身に覚えのない記録
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;その後、調査によって不正プログラムの存在が確認され、フミカは自分の PC が第三者に悪用されていた可能性を説明することができた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ただし、パソコンを調べたからといって、過去の通信をすべて解明できるわけではない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;いつ感染したのか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;その間に、どれほどの通信が中継されたのか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;フミカの PC を経由して、どのような犯罪が行われたのか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;すべてが明らかになるとは限らない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;彼女は PC の利用を停止し、専門家に相談して不正プログラムへの対応を進めた。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;それでも、金融機関などの通信記録には、確かにフミカの自宅の IP アドレスが残っている。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;彼女は犯罪をしていない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;しかし、彼女の家から犯罪に関係する通信が行われたという記録は存在している。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


犯罪に関係するつもりなどなかった
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;私たちは、自分のパソコンやスマートフォンを、自分のために使っていると思っている。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ネット通販で買い物をする。動画を見る。仕事をする。銀行の残高を確認する。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;その裏側で、自分の端末が何をしているのか、すべてを把握している人はほとんどいない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;インストールしたアプリが、どのような通信を行っているのか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;無料サービスが、どのような条件で提供されているのか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;自分の機器が、誰かの通信を中継していないか。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;それらを四六時中確認することはできない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;けれど、端末が正常に動いていることと、自分の意思どおりに動いていることは同じではない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;「自分には盗まれて困る情報などない」&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そう思っていても、攻撃者が必要としているのは、あなたの情報とは限らない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;あなたのパソコン、あなたの回線、あなたの IP アドレス。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;それらが、あなたの知らない誰かに利用価値のあるものだったとしたら。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;フミカは、パソコンを開くたびに、以前より少しだけ通信のことを気にするようになった。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;けれど、画面にはいつものデスクトップが表示される。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;何も変わっていない。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;そして、何も変わっていないように見えることが、いちばん不気味だった。&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;参考資料&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;U.S. Department of Justice, "911 S5 Botnet Dismantled and Its Administrator Arrested in Coordinated International Operation" (May 29, 2024).&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/911-s5-botnet-dismantled-and-its-administrator-arrested-coordinated-international-operation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/911-s5-botnet-dismantled-and-its-administrator-arrested-coordinated-international-operation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Texas, "Affidavit in Support of Application for Seizure Warrant", &lt;em&gt;In the Matter of the Seizure of Property Associated with 911 S5&lt;/em&gt;, Case No. 4:24-mj-00365-KPJ, filed May 21, 2024.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.justice.gov/opa/media/1353521/dl?inline=" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.justice.gov/opa/media/1353521/dl?inline=&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3), "Guidance on the 911 S5 Residential Proxy Service" (May 29, 2024).&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2024/PSA240529" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2024/PSA240529&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI), "Evading Residential Proxy Networks: Protecting Your Devices from Becoming a Tool for Criminals" (March 12, 2026).&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2026/PSA260312" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.ic3.gov/PSA/2026/PSA260312&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;国立研究開発法人情報通信研究機構 (NICT), 『家庭用 IoT 機器を悪用するレジデンシャルプロキシの現状をまとめたファクトシートを公表』(2026 年 7 月 21 日)。総務省・警察庁との共同公表。&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www1.nict.go.jp/press/2026/07/21-1.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www1.nict.go.jp/press/2026/07/21-1.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;独立行政法人情報処理推進機構 (IPA), 『企業組織を狙うメールを悪用した攻撃の手口とその対策』(2026 年 8 月 3 日)。ソーシャルエンジニアリングの説明を参照。&lt;br&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.ipa.go.jp/security/support/abuse_email.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.ipa.go.jp/security/support/abuse_email.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;本記事のフミカ、警察官の訪問、金融機関への不正アクセスとの関係、感染に至る個別の経緯は、公開されている捜査資料や公的機関の注意喚起をもとにした架空の再構成です。911 S5 というボットネットとその摘発は実在の事案ですが、本記事の物語が特定の被害者や実際の捜査事案を再現したものではありません。&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftxgxdb6rvgi6uw4xnj2v.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftxgxdb6rvgi6uw4xnj2v.png" alt=" " width="800" height="379"&gt;&lt;/a&gt; The Problem: Managing Complex Book Data Without Prop Drilling&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I started building &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://ayubooks.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AYUBOOKS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, a digital cataloging and reading platform inspired by Letterboxd, the initial prototype relied heavily on local component state (&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;). Everything worked smoothly with just a handful of books. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, as the catalog grew and I introduced real-world features—such as instant search filtering, category tagging, tracking reading statuses, and user reviews—three major bottlenecks quickly emerged:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Severe Prop Drilling:&lt;/strong&gt; Passing book lists, search queries, and state-updating functions down through multiple layers of nested components (from the main layout to search bars, filter sidebars, and individual book cards) made the codebase rigid and hard to refactor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State Synchronization Bugs:&lt;/strong&gt; Updating a book's status in one view (like marking it as "Read" inside a modal) didn't always reflect immediately in the main catalog grid or user statistics without messy workarounds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unnecessary Re-renders:&lt;/strong&gt; Every keystroke in the search bar triggered state updates at the top level, causing large portions of the UI tree to re-render and making the filtering experience feel sluggish.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To keep &lt;strong&gt;AYUBOOKS&lt;/strong&gt; fast, scalable, and maintainable, I needed a centralized, predictable state management solution—which led me to refactor the core architecture using &lt;strong&gt;Redux Toolkit&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Architecture: Centralizing State with Redux Toolkit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of reaching for boilerplate-heavy classic Redux, I integrated &lt;strong&gt;Redux Toolkit (RTK)&lt;/strong&gt; alongside &lt;strong&gt;React 19&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Vite&lt;/strong&gt;. RTK gave me a single source of truth for the entire book catalog while keeping the logic modular and easy to debug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is how I structured the Redux store folder inside the project:&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
text
src/
├── app/
│   └── store.js          # Configures the central Redux store
└── features/
└── books/
├── booksSlice.js # Manages catalog data, search query, and status updates
└── BookList.jsx  # Renders the filtered book grid cleanly`// src/features/books/booksSlice.js
import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const initialState = {
items: [], // Populated with book objects { id, title, author, category, status }
searchQuery: '',
selectedCategory: 'All',
};

const booksSlice = createSlice({
name: 'books',
initialState,
reducers: {
setBooks: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.items = action.payload;
},
setSearchQuery: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.searchQuery = action.payload;
},
setCategoryFilter: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.selectedCategory = action.payload;
},
toggleReadStatus: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
const book = state.items.find((b) =&amp;gt; b.id === action.payload);
if (book) {
book.status = book.status === 'Read' ? 'Want to Read' : 'Read';
}
},
},
});

export const { 
setBooks, 
setSearchQuery, 
setCategoryFilter, 
toggleReadStatus 
} = booksSlice.actions;

export default booksSlice.reducer;`
// src/features/books/booksSlice.js
import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const initialState = {
items: [], // Populated with book objects { id, title, author, category, status }
searchQuery: '',
selectedCategory: 'All',
};

const booksSlice = createSlice({
name: 'books',
initialState,
reducers: {
setBooks: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.items = action.payload;
},
setSearchQuery: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.searchQuery = action.payload;
},
setCategoryFilter: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.selectedCategory = action.payload;
},
toggleReadStatus: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
const book = state.items.find((b) =&amp;gt; b.id === action.payload);
if (book) {
book.status = book.status === 'Read' ? 'Want to Read' : 'Read';
}
},
},
});

export const { 
setBooks, 
setSearchQuery, 
setCategoryFilter, 
toggleReadStatus 
} = booksSlice.actions;

export default booksSlice.reducer;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftxgxdb6rvgi6uw4xnj2v.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ftxgxdb6rvgi6uw4xnj2v.png" alt=" " width="800" height="379"&gt;&lt;/a&gt; The Problem: Managing Complex Book Data Without Prop Drilling&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I started building &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://ayubooks.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AYUBOOKS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, a digital cataloging and reading platform inspired by Letterboxd, the initial prototype relied heavily on local component state (&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;). Everything worked smoothly with just a handful of books. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, as the catalog grew and I introduced real-world features—such as instant search filtering, category tagging, tracking reading statuses, and user reviews—three major bottlenecks quickly emerged:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Severe Prop Drilling:&lt;/strong&gt; Passing book lists, search queries, and state-updating functions down through multiple layers of nested components (from the main layout to search bars, filter sidebars, and individual book cards) made the codebase rigid and hard to refactor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State Synchronization Bugs:&lt;/strong&gt; Updating a book's status in one view (like marking it as "Read" inside a modal) didn't always reflect immediately in the main catalog grid or user statistics without messy workarounds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unnecessary Re-renders:&lt;/strong&gt; Every keystroke in the search bar triggered state updates at the top level, causing large portions of the UI tree to re-render and making the filtering experience feel sluggish.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To keep &lt;strong&gt;AYUBOOKS&lt;/strong&gt; fast, scalable, and maintainable, I needed a centralized, predictable state management solution—which led me to refactor the core architecture using &lt;strong&gt;Redux Toolkit&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Architecture: Centralizing State with Redux Toolkit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of reaching for boilerplate-heavy classic Redux, I integrated &lt;strong&gt;Redux Toolkit (RTK)&lt;/strong&gt; alongside &lt;strong&gt;React 19&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Vite&lt;/strong&gt;. RTK gave me a single source of truth for the entire book catalog while keeping the logic modular and easy to debug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is how I structured the Redux store folder inside the project:&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
text
src/
├── app/
│   └── store.js          # Configures the central Redux store
└── features/
└── books/
├── booksSlice.js # Manages catalog data, search query, and status updates
└── BookList.jsx  # Renders the filtered book grid cleanly`// src/features/books/booksSlice.js
import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const initialState = {
items: [], // Populated with book objects { id, title, author, category, status }
searchQuery: '',
selectedCategory: 'All',
};

const booksSlice = createSlice({
name: 'books',
initialState,
reducers: {
setBooks: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.items = action.payload;
},
setSearchQuery: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.searchQuery = action.payload;
},
setCategoryFilter: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
state.selectedCategory = action.payload;
},
toggleReadStatus: (state, action) =&amp;gt; {
const book = state.items.find((b) =&amp;gt; b.id === action.payload);
if (book) {
book.status = book.status === 'Read' ? 'Want to Read' : 'Read';
}
},
},
});

export const { 
setBooks, 
setSearchQuery, 
setCategoryFilter, 
toggleReadStatus 
} = booksSlice.actions;

export default booksSlice.reducer;`
